# Read through selectors

## Context

`app/src/selectors.ts` holds pure read helpers (`visibleTasks`,
`remainingCount`) so that derived/filter logic lives in one place. Consumers read
through these instead of re-implementing filtering over `state.tasks`, which
keeps read logic consistent and testable.

## Rule

- In consumer code (UI, index, new modules), derive views via selectors in
  `app/src/selectors.ts` — do not filter/reduce `state.tasks` inline.
- When a feature needs a new derived read, add a pure named selector to
  `selectors.ts` (with a colocated test) rather than duplicating logic.
- The reducer (`reducer.ts`) and the selectors themselves legitimately operate on
  `state.tasks` — this rule targets consumers, not those two files.

## How to verify

1. Outside `reducer.ts` and `selectors.ts`,
   `grep -rn "state.tasks.filter\|state.tasks.map" app/src` → prefer a selector.
2. New derived reads appear as named exports in `selectors.ts` with a test.
3. `cd app && npm test` is green.
